# Reveal Codes

A drafting editor for court filings that shows where every word came from and catches fake citations before a judge does.

Try it at https://reveal-codes.gowthamsarveswaran.com. Built for Kiroween 2026, Resurrection category.

<img src="docs/images/editor.png" alt="The editor with a verified motion: the page above, the codes pane below with four failing citations as red blocks, and the Issues list on the right">

## Why it exists

Courts keep sanctioning filings that cite cases which do not exist. One public count stood at 2,266 such decisions on 5 November 2026, and 1,305 of them involved people representing themselves (AI Hallucination Cases Database, Damien Charlotin, CC BY 4.0). Federal courts have answered with rules about AI in filings, each in its own words.

WordPerfect 5.1 for DOS, in 1989, had a key that split the screen and showed every hidden code in a document: Alt-F3, Reveal Codes. Lawyers trusted it because nothing was hidden. This project brings that screen back for what is hidden in a draft today: which words a person typed, which a model wrote, and whether the cases it cites are real. Reveal Codes was a feature of WordPerfect; this project is not affiliated with Corel.

## What it does

**It records provenance instead of guessing it.** Every character carries a code for how it arrived: `HUM` typed, `PST` pasted, `AI` written by the assistant, `AI>HUM` assistant text the author then edited. Nothing is inferred by a detector.

**It checks citations and quotations.** Verify looks up every legal citation on CourtListener and compares each quotation with the text of the opinion it is attributed to. A citation that cannot be found, or a quotation the opinion does not contain, becomes a red code and a blocking item. A citation must also name the case it points to: if the case name written before it does not match the opinion CourtListener finds, it is reported too.

**There is a page for checking citations alone.** `/check` takes pasted or opened text, lists every citation and quotation with its status and one plain sentence on what to do next, and needs no account. It reads from 320px wide, so it works on a phone.

**Alt-F3 shows everything.** The codes pane opens under the page and prints the same text with every code inline. The margin numbers are the document's line numbers, the same ones the status line and the Sentinel use.

**A draft can be opened from a file.** A draft can be opened from a `.docx`, `.txt` or `.md` file, and an exported `.docx` is reopened with its record; a file changed after it was exported is opened as pasted text.

**Export is gated.** A filing with red codes will not export until each one is fixed or overridden with a written reason, and the reason is printed. The .docx carries the filing, an AI-Use and Verification Statement, and a Provenance Appendix that can be read back by machine.

<img src="docs/images/statement.png" alt="The AI-Use and Verification Statement in the print view: the court's rule cited, a table of citations with their status, a table of quotations, and the override reasons">

**The statement states the record; the author states the rest.** The statement reports what the tool recorded: how text entered, which citations and quotations were checked, which overrides were written. Text that was pasted or imported has no recorded origin beyond that, so the author makes a declaration about it, and the statement prints it as the author's own. The tool never asserts on its own that no AI was used.

**The statement follows the court.** Pick the court or judge and the statement takes that instrument's citation, title and required elements. The details are in \[docs/CERTIFICATION.md\](docs/CERTIFICATION.md).

<img src="docs/images/export.png" alt="The Export dialog with Northern District of Texas Local Civil Rule 7.2(f) selected, its quoted language, and four blocking items">

## Demo path

On the hosted site citations are checked live. Locally, with no token set, the app runs on recorded responses.

1. Open the site, or run `pnpm dev` and open http://localhost:3000.
2. On the empty page, select Load demo motion, the second sample button; the Avianca sample, Load Mata v. Avianca, is the first offered. The text enters as one `PST` span.
3. The codes pane is already open on a first visit; Alt-F3 hides and shows it.
4. Select Verify. Two citations are found. Three are not, and one quotation is not in its opinion: they turn red in the pane, are underlined on the page, and are listed under Issues. Go jumps to each.
5. Select Export. Choose a court, for example N.D. Tex. The dialog says how many blocking items remain and keeps Download .docx disabled.
6. Make the declaration about the pasted text: choose Declaration_Choice_AI (generative AI was used to prepare some or all of it) and type a named tool in the tool field.
7. Select Override… on an item and write a reason, or correct the text and Verify again. When nothing blocks, Download .docx produces the filing with its statement and appendix. The statement prints the declaration as the author's statement, with the tool you named.

Open is the other way in beside Load demo motion: choose Open to start from a `.docx`, `.txt` or `.md` file of your own.

To see the case-name check, select Load Mata v. Avianca, the first sample button, on the empty page instead of the demo motion, then Verify. The sample buttons appear only while the draft is empty. To switch samples, press New first.

On a phone, open `/check`, paste or open your draft, and select Check citations.

Paste text of your own on the hosted site to see real citations found and invented ones caught.

## The dataset

`data/standing-orders.json` holds 41 federal instruments on generative AI in court filings, across 27 courts: local rules, general orders, and judges' standing orders. 36 are in force. Each entry was read from the court's own published text and records what the instrument requires, whom it applies to, one quoted sentence and the source URL. We found no official list of them. The method and fields are in \[data/README.md\](data/README.md). The dataset is released under CC0.

The instruments are also listed, by court, at https://reveal-codes.gowthamsarveswaran.com/courts.

## Use the checker inside Kiro

The citation checker is also packaged as a Kiro Power that works in any workspace: import `powers/citation-check` from a folder or from GitHub, and ask Kiro to check the citations in a file. It runs on recorded responses with no setup and checks live when `COURTLISTENER_TOKEN` is set; see \[the Power's README\](powers/citation-check/README.md).

## Sentinel

The Sentinel re-checks exported filings. It reads each `.docx` in a folder, reads the Provenance Appendix back out of it, asks CourtListener the same questions the editor asked, and reports where the answer differs from the record the file carries. It reports these checks:

- Body edited: the filing text no longer matches the text the Appendix records.
- Citation changed: a citation's verification status differs from the recorded one, or now points at another decision.
- Quote changed: a quotation's check result differs from the recorded one.
- Override no longer needed: an override sits on an issue that is no longer an issue.
- Recheck unavailable: CourtListener could not be reached or throttled the request, so the record was left as is.
- Unreadable: the file is not a Reveal Codes export, or could not be read or parsed.

```
pnpm sentinel sweep ~/Filings
```

The report is Markdown on stdout. Its second line states the mode, `fixtures` or `live`. Only citation strings and cluster ids go to CourtListener; no draft text is sent. The Sentinel reads four environment variables:

- `COURTLISTENER_TOKEN`: token for live lookups; without it the Sentinel runs on recorded fixtures.
- `REVEAL_CODES_FIXTURES_ONLY`: set to `1` to force fixtures mode even when a token is set.
- `REVEAL_CODES_CACHE_DIR`: directory for the lookup cache; the default is `~/.reveal-codes/cache`, shared with the web app.
- `COURTLISTENER_BASE_URL`: overrides the CourtListener API base URL.

The Kiro Crew app that schedules the Sentinel lives in a separate repository: https://github.com/gowtham0992/reveal-codes-sentinel. The app was installed in Kiro Crew 0.12.0 and its nightly job run once over three sample filings on 2026-11-09; the run swept all three and reported the one edited after export.

[AI:11.2 README section "Reveal Codes for your own repository" 2026-11-21T18:48:45Z.1{t=2026-11-21T18:57:43.165Z}]## Reveal Codes for your own repository

The Provenance Power records which text in your source files the Kiro agent changed, and prints a file with its codes in front of the text. The record is made by Kiro's own hooks when the agent works, not guessed from the text. No prompt text is stored, and nothing leaves the machine.

### Read a file with its codes

Run these from the workspace root. Each command has two forms; the second runs where the wrapper cannot.

- `.kiro/provenance/reveal <file>` or `node .kiro/provenance/provenance.mjs reveal <file>` prints the file with its codes.
- `.kiro/provenance/reveal --summary \[path\]` or `node .kiro/provenance/provenance.mjs reveal --summary \[path\]` prints the share of each origin, the longest untouched agent stretches and the agent windows.
- `.kiro/provenance/reveal --check \[--strict\]` or `node .kiro/provenance/provenance.mjs reveal --check \[--strict\]` checks the records for damage.
- `reveal --record` (in either form) brings the records up to date by hand.

In this repository, `pnpm reveal <file>` runs the same command from the sources.

